        double global_sum[K * D] = {0};
        long global_cnt[K] = {0};

        #pragma omp parallel
        {
            double local_sum[K * D] = {0};
            long local_cnt[K] = {0};

            #pragma omp for schedule(guided)
            for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                int best = 0;
                double bd = 1e300;
                const double *p = &pts[i * D];
                for (int c = 0; c < K; c++) {
                    double d = 0.0;
                    const double *cen = &cent[c * D];
                    for (int k = 0; k < D; k++) {
                        double t = p[k] - cen[k];
                        d += t * t;
                    }
                    if (d < bd) {
                        bd = d;
                        best = c;
                    }
                }
                label[i] = best;
                local_cnt[best]++;
                for (int k = 0; k < D; k++) {
                    local_sum[best * D + k] += p[k];
                }
            }

            #pragma omp critical
            {
                for (int c = 0; c < K * D; c++) global_sum[c] += local_sum[c];
                for (int c = 0; c < K; c++) global_cnt[c] += local_cnt[c];
            }
        }

        for (int c = 0; c < K; c++) {
            if (global_cnt[c] > 0) {
                for (int k = 0; k < D; k++) {
                    cent[c * D + k] = global_sum[c * D + k] / global_cnt[c];
                }
            }
        }
    }
    ```

    *   P-cores vs E-cores: `schedule(guided)` is good.
    *   False sharing: `local_sum` and `local_cnt` are on each thread's stack, so no false sharing during the main loop.
    *   Memory bandwidth: `pts` is read once per iteration. `cent` is read $n \times K$ times but fits in L1. This is compute-bound ($O(nKD)$).
    *   `#pragma omp parallel` overhead: 10 iterations $\times$ (parallel region creation) is negligible.
    *   Array access: Use pointers to avoid repeated index multiplication (`p = &pts[i*D]`).

